## What

The **first place the compiled table replaces hand logic.** Deletes the hand-written `subset_punct_tk` + `is_subset_punct` + `scan_compound_op` (~92 lines) from the reference byte-scanner and calls the **generated** `scan_operator` (from #937) instead. One trie of byte-branches, generated from `operator_rules()`, now does the single-char punctuation *and* the compound-operator maximal munch that were two open-coded functions.

**"Compile the grammar, don't interpret it."**

## The new `_` arm

```avra
if b == SLASH && s.nxt() == SLASH {
    …doc-comment token…            // a lexer MODE, stays hand-written
} else if b == SQUOTE {
    …char literal…                 // mode
} else if b == DQUOTE {
    …string literal…               // mode
} else {
    let m = scan_operator(s.bytes, s.pos)   // GENERATED
    if m.len == 0 { return "<out-of-subset:char>" }
    else { advance m.len; emit m.tk }
}
```

- Modes (doc-comment, char, string) stay hand-written — they open a *mode*, not a fixed token.
- Everything else — every operator + single-char punct — is the generated scanner. Bare `/` (division) falls through to it (→ `Slash`); only `//`-lead doc comments are special-cased.
- `len == 0` = a lead `scan_operator` doesn't own → the same out-of-subset marker as before.

## Proof

Faithful drop-in, proven by the **existing oracle** — `scan_subset_test`'s 9 blocks (`scan_subset(x) == lex_dump(x)`) all still pass, byte-identical to the hand lexer across identifiers, punctuation, compound operators, adjacency, the `/` family, char/string literals, and out-of-subset markers.

## Safety

- `scan_subset` is a non-production reference ⇒ compiler **IR byte-identical**. `diff-test`: selfhost 926335 lines + 22 corpus, 0 skipped.
- Pruned the now-unused operator/punct byte-constant imports (`PERCENT`/`AMP`/…/`TILDE`).

**Stacked** on #937 (base = `claude/avra-p1-scanner-gen`); this diff is the wiring alone. Part of epic **t-47hc**, Phase 1 (t-47hc.2). This validates the generated scanner as a drop-in in a real scanning loop through the oracle — the precondition for eventually wiring it into the production hand lexer.

